01:25:17,262 --> 01:25:19,942
systems and digitization projects, and

485
01:25:20,822 --> 01:25:24,562
then COVID got us, and during COVID, I became a birder,

486
01:25:25,182 --> 01:25:28,922
a really avid birder, and I got so hooked that I

487
01:25:28,962 --> 01:25:32,802
decided to enroll in a master's program at Cornell University, the

488
01:25:32,882 --> 01:25:35,522
home of the Lab of Ornithology.

489
01:25:37,082 --> 01:25:40,562
And as you can probably tell, I was not the average student.

490
01:25:40,942 --> 01:25:42,222
The typical student, I mean.

491
01:25:44,102 --> 01:25:46,442
At the university, I met Vanja Rover.

492
01:25:46,782 --> 01:25:50,582
He's the curator of birds at the Cornell University Museum of Vertebrates,

493
01:25:51,262 --> 01:25:54,762
and I began volunteering in the oology collection.

494
01:25:55,162 --> 01:25:59,122
So this was a new field for me. Oology is the study

495
01:25:59,182 --> 01:26:00,882
and collecting of birds' eggs,

496
01:26:01,682 --> 01:26:05,522
and I was surprised to hear that it was a very popular hobby 100 years

497
01:26:05,602 --> 01:26:05,822
ago.

498
01:26:08,282 --> 01:26:11,622
So I started working with the egg sets and many of these cards

499
01:26:12,542 --> 01:26:16,522
that got me hooked immediately. This is for a Baltimore oriole nest

500
01:26:17,582 --> 01:26:21,522
in Ontario, 1937, and it describes the nest as greyish

501
01:26:21,602 --> 01:26:25,562
hemp and fibers, wool, coarse and fine grasses,

502
01:26:25,922 --> 01:26:28,622
fine strips of bark, and horse hair.

503
01:26:30,222 --> 01:26:33,582
Vanja Rover, he uses these cards to study how nest

504
01:26:33,642 --> 01:26:36,102
materials have changed throughout the years.

505
01:26:36,662 --> 01:26:39,862
So it's very important information, but it's trapped in these cards.

506
01:26:40,562 --> 01:26:44,361
And these cards matter because today, nests have changed a lot.

507
01:26:44,962 --> 01:26:48,862
This is a photo of a Baltimore oriole nest that Vanya photographed.

508
01:26:49,342 --> 01:26:53,142
It's woven with strips of blue plastic, so things have

509
01:26:53,182 --> 01:26:57,002
changed. But the change is only visible against all that

510
01:26:57,042 --> 01:27:00,902
data trapped in those cards. So museums worldwide are

511
01:27:00,962 --> 01:27:04,902
holding maybe two million exits, and many have cards like that

512
01:27:04,942 --> 01:27:05,102
one.

513
01:27:06,262 --> 01:27:09,862
Some digital catalogs may have digitized the metadata, but not the

514
01:27:09,902 --> 01:27:11,522
transcription of the nest.

515
01:27:12,682 --> 01:27:16,342
So that is information that hasn't been transcribed, and that's

516
01:27:16,382 --> 01:27:16,742
both

517
01:27:17,562 --> 01:27:21,022
a research opportunity and a big transcription challenge.

518
01:27:22,062 --> 01:27:23,902
So in 2024,

519
01:27:24,822 --> 01:27:28,422
I was working with a collection from Robert Lyle, who was a collector in

520
01:27:28,462 --> 01:27:28,922
Tennessee.

521
01:27:29,782 --> 01:27:33,142
And I decided to test whether GPT-4o could help.

522
01:27:33,802 --> 01:27:37,302
I photographed 262 cards from that collection

523
01:27:37,782 --> 01:27:38,742
using my iPhone,

524
01:27:39,562 --> 01:27:41,302
and I sent them through GPT-4o.

525
01:27:42,642 --> 01:27:46,622
GPT-4o transcribed the whole card, but I will focus only on

526
01:27:46,662 --> 01:27:50,282
the nest descriptions since that's the rich part of data that we want to work on.

527
01:27:51,962 --> 01:27:55,942
So out of 262 transcripts, 193 were

528
01:27:56,002 --> 01:27:59,902
exact. That's three out of four. And I thought, "Wow, this

529
01:27:59,942 --> 01:28:00,462
is amazing."

530
01:28:01,302 --> 01:28:05,122
The typed cards were nearly perfect, so let's focus only on the

531
01:28:05,162 --> 01:28:08,822
handwritten cards. Two in three of those were

532
01:28:08,862 --> 01:28:12,462
exact. And I thought, "Wow, that's great," but it's also

533
01:28:12,502 --> 01:28:14,162
frustrating because

534
01:28:15,202 --> 01:28:19,042
there's no way I can know which one is correct and which one needs

535
01:28:19,202 --> 01:28:19,722
changes

536
01:28:20,542 --> 01:28:22,342
if I don't open each one and check it.

537
01:28:23,422 --> 01:28:27,102
So my question was very naively, how can the model

538
01:28:27,222 --> 01:28:27,622
tell me

539
01:28:28,522 --> 01:28:30,142
which one is right, which one is wrong?

540
01:28:31,222 --> 01:28:32,442
And I went reading the manual,

541
01:28:33,662 --> 01:28:35,522
and I found this setting, logprops.

542
01:28:36,502 --> 01:28:40,262
I'm just curious, how many of you have used or heard about logprops?

543
01:28:41,642 --> 01:28:42,542
Okay, a couple of you.

544
01:28:44,222 --> 01:28:48,202
The log probabilities is a signal that some models can give you.

545
01:28:48,942 --> 01:28:52,522
If you set logprops equal true like I did here, the model will

546
01:28:52,562 --> 01:28:56,062
report how sure it was of every token that it wrote.

547
01:28:56,782 --> 01:29:00,642
And also, in this case, I'm asking for the five top

548
01:29:01,002 --> 01:29:02,602
alternatives that it considered.

549
01:29:03,242 --> 01:29:07,002
By the way, a token, as probably most of you know, is simply a

550
01:29:07,082 --> 01:29:08,482
word or a part of a word.

551
01:29:09,462 --> 01:29:12,942
So let's look at an example. This is a ring-billed gull card

552
01:29:13,782 --> 01:29:17,502
for a gull colony in Oregon, 1939. Describes the nest

553
01:29:18,022 --> 01:29:21,782
as made of bits of tulle and feathers, and perhaps

554
01:29:21,922 --> 01:29:23,982
2,000 pairs nesting there.

555
01:29:26,422 --> 01:29:28,982
So the model tells us what it almost wrote.

556
01:29:29,082 --> 01:29:30,982
When the model wrote feathers, it was

557
01:29:31,182 --> 01:29:33,582
99.997%

558
01:29:34,962 --> 01:29:35,382
certain.

559
01:29:36,582 --> 01:29:37,602
It didn't hesitate at all.

560
01:29:38,662 --> 01:29:42,562
But for colony, it wrote 7,000 and it was only

561
01:29:42,802 --> 01:29:43,942
58% sure.

562
01:29:45,422 --> 01:29:48,522
So the other option, 2,000, was behind it.

563
01:29:48,762 --> 01:29:52,222
It was the second top one at 27% of

564
01:29:52,282 --> 01:29:52,802
certainty.

565
01:29:53,622 --> 01:29:56,902
So this means that the model hesitated exactly where it went wrong.

566
01:29:57,842 --> 01:30:00,782
That seemed like the perfect signal, so I dug deeper.

567
01:30:03,022 --> 01:30:06,842
First, I decided to call a token hesitant when the

568
01:30:06,882 --> 01:30:10,362
top choice is less than 2.7 times as likely as a runner-up.

569
01:30:11,222 --> 01:30:14,702
7,000 is only 2.1 times as likely, so it's hesitant.

570
01:30:14,862 --> 01:30:18,702
But nesting versus resting was hesitant too and

571
01:30:18,942 --> 01:30:19,802
still correct.

572
01:30:20,602 --> 01:30:24,422
So hesitation may mark uncertainty, but not necessarily

573
01:30:24,522 --> 01:30:26,382
errors, which is what we were after.

574
01:30:29,502 --> 01:30:31,502
So this is every token on the gull card.

575
01:30:31,922 --> 01:30:35,282
Fifty-five tokens are confident and only two are hesitant.

576
01:30:35,682 --> 01:30:39,482
So I defined card hesitation score as the number of hesitant tokens.

577
01:30:39,902 --> 01:30:42,762
So for this card, the card hesitation score is two.

578
01:30:44,082 --> 01:30:47,862
And here you can see all 201 handwritten cards by hesitation

579
01:30:47,942 --> 01:30:51,042
score. Color shows how many words I corrected.

580
01:30:51,682 --> 01:30:55,542
A few cards on the left had no hesitation, but still needed

581
01:30:55,602 --> 01:30:58,702
fixes. The model makes confident errors.

582
01:30:59,262 --> 01:31:01,962
And a very few hesitant cards were still exact.

583
01:31:02,582 --> 01:31:05,722
But look where the dark dots gather, on the right long tail.

584
01:31:06,502 --> 01:31:09,582
Let's look at the furthest one, the darkest dot at 13.

585
01:31:10,482 --> 01:31:13,422
It's for a gray-tailed grackle set, Texas,

586
01:31:13,862 --> 01:31:17,582
1938. Pre-printed dots, no punctuation,

587
01:31:17,802 --> 01:31:21,422
kind of messy writing. Words like stump and inner

588
01:31:21,482 --> 01:31:23,302
lining that are really hard to read.

589
01:31:24,002 --> 01:31:27,882
The card says about 14 feet up, and the model wrote almost 4

590
01:31:27,942 --> 01:31:28,202
feet.

591
01:31:29,022 --> 01:31:30,942
This is a card for an expert reviewer.

592
01:31:31,942 --> 01:31:35,662
So I defined three lanes. A light lane for zero or one

593
01:31:35,702 --> 01:31:39,522
hesitant tokens, a standard lane, two to four, five and up.

594
01:31:39,742 --> 01:31:43,042
Cards like the grackle card, difficult ones for the experts.

595
01:31:43,682 --> 01:31:47,422
Expert lane cards took me much longer, so I measured the time.

596
01:31:47,862 --> 01:31:51,802
My review tool read each transcript aloud while

597
01:31:51,902 --> 01:31:54,842
I followed the card. If it matched, I pressed Enter.

598
01:31:55,342 --> 01:31:59,002
If not, I correct it, and the tool timed my correction time.

599
01:32:00,342 --> 01:32:04,142
Median correction time is under a second in the light and standard

600
01:32:04,222 --> 01:32:06,762
lanes. Eleven seconds in the expert lane.

601
01:32:06,942 --> 01:32:10,762
So hesitation may not signal correctness, but it seems to track

602
01:32:10,962 --> 01:32:11,902
review effort.

603
01:32:12,842 --> 01:32:16,542
And this held very well on 2,303 new

604
01:32:16,582 --> 01:32:20,082
handwritten cards from the Western Foundation of Vertebrate Zoology,

605
01:32:20,742 --> 01:32:24,322
also transcribed by GPT-4o. A different mix of collectors and

606
01:32:24,402 --> 01:32:27,562
hands, harder cards. I used the same three lanes.

607
01:32:27,802 --> 01:32:29,862
Median correction times are stated there.

608
01:32:30,522 --> 01:32:34,462
And so these three lanes and the step-up in the

609
01:32:34,522 --> 01:32:37,302
review time isn't a quirk of one collection.

610
01:32:37,462 --> 01:32:39,702
The lanes still seem to sort the work.

611
01:32:42,366 --> 01:32:45,946
The practical result is that for both collections, the expert lane

612
01:32:46,026 --> 01:32:49,466
contains about a fifth of the cards and takes up half to

613
01:32:49,546 --> 01:32:50,966
two-thirds of the correction time.

614
01:32:51,366 --> 01:32:51,686
So if you

615
01:32:52,926 --> 01:32:56,906
time a sample of your cards, you could estimate how long the

616
01:32:56,946 --> 01:33:00,826
rest will take and see which cards need your most experienced reader

617
01:33:00,986 --> 01:33:04,746
and the most time. Of course, models have moved on

618
01:33:04,946 --> 01:33:05,926
since I started this.

619
01:33:07,486 --> 01:33:10,306
GPT-4o read two in three exactly right.

620
01:33:10,466 --> 01:33:13,646
In my tests with the newer models, Gemini 3.1 Pro,

621
01:33:14,226 --> 01:33:14,866
Opus 5,

622
01:33:15,966 --> 01:33:19,746
those get nearly nine in 10 correctly, but they don't

623
01:33:19,806 --> 01:33:23,486
expose log probs. So does any of this still matter

624
01:33:23,526 --> 01:33:23,866
today?

625
01:33:25,206 --> 01:33:29,046
I think it does, because nine in 10 still doesn't tell you

626
01:33:29,206 --> 01:33:31,066
which one wasn't correct.

627
01:33:32,146 --> 01:33:36,046
Gemini 3.1 Pro and Opus 5 write exactly the same thing for

628
01:33:36,266 --> 01:33:38,846
98.5% of the words.

629
01:33:39,646 --> 01:33:42,906
When these models agree, I found that they are right about

630
01:33:43,246 --> 01:33:47,146
997 times out of 1,000. So still

631
01:33:47,186 --> 01:33:47,926
not always.

632
01:33:48,786 --> 01:33:52,686
Where the ink is ambiguous, both wrote clump for stump,

633
01:33:52,986 --> 01:33:54,726
but you see it's really hard to read, right?

634
01:33:58,946 --> 01:34:01,746
And this is also where GPT-4o hesitated.

635
01:34:02,326 --> 01:34:05,146
Both still modernized the spelling for bulrushes.

636
01:34:05,386 --> 01:34:06,726
There was nothing to do about that.

637
01:34:07,386 --> 01:34:10,886
So agreement helps sort the cards, but it doesn't certify them.

638
01:34:11,266 --> 01:34:15,126
Somebody still has to check, and the job should be to make that

639
01:34:15,186 --> 01:34:16,126
checking easier.

640
01:34:17,606 --> 01:34:18,526
So what did we learn?

641
01:34:19,646 --> 01:34:23,586
First, models already read most of these cards well, but none of them

642
01:34:23,626 --> 01:34:26,846
tells you which ones are right. Second, hesitation

643
01:34:27,366 --> 01:34:30,666
doesn't catch every error, but it predicts the review work.

644
01:34:31,046 --> 01:34:34,846
A fifth of the cards take half the time or more, and you can see which fifth

645
01:34:35,186 --> 01:34:38,986
before you start. Third, the best models don't report their

646
01:34:39,006 --> 01:34:42,966
probabilities, but GPT-4o or any other open-weight model you

647
01:34:43,026 --> 01:34:44,786
run yourself still can.

648
01:34:45,966 --> 01:34:47,426
I will be working on this further.

649
01:34:48,586 --> 01:34:52,446
In my library years, I learned that the key to digitizing

650
01:34:52,506 --> 01:34:56,426
was not the scanning, it was the people describing, checking,

651
01:34:56,686 --> 01:34:59,266
correcting the records, and it's the same here.

652
01:34:59,826 --> 01:35:02,186
The question is not, can I trust the transcript?

653
01:35:02,626 --> 01:35:04,786
It's where should the human effort go?

654
01:35:05,566 --> 01:35:09,126
And two million cards, like that 1937 Oriole card,

655
01:35:09,526 --> 01:35:11,506
are waiting for us. Thank you.

656
01:35:17,526 --> 01:35:19,546
Great timing, Greta. Thank you so much.

657
01:35:19,666 --> 01:35:20,706
Okay.

658
01:35:20,746 --> 01:35:22,646
We will have a couple of questions.

659
01:35:22,726 --> 01:35:22,866
Okay.

660
01:35:37,206 --> 01:35:39,306
Thank you for that amazing presentation.

661
01:35:39,506 --> 01:35:39,646
Thank you.

662
01:35:39,686 --> 01:35:42,646
What a wonderful method. It was really incredible.

663
01:35:43,166 --> 01:35:46,746
It's too bad that log probs is not exposed anymore.

664
01:35:46,826 --> 01:35:50,666
Have you tried asking the model to rate its own confidence,

665
01:35:50,786 --> 01:35:54,546
and do you have faith in that as a measure of its confidence?

666
01:35:55,066 --> 01:35:59,006
Yeah. At the beginning, I tried that, and it's not something that the model can do

667
01:35:59,046 --> 01:35:59,806
consistently.

668
01:36:00,686 --> 01:36:00,846
Yeah.

669
01:36:01,606 --> 01:36:05,206
Is there any alternative? And do you know if smaller

670
01:36:05,286 --> 01:36:08,006
models are still able to report those probabilities?

671
01:36:08,546 --> 01:36:12,446
For now, Gemini 2.1 Flash, I think still does it, but

672
01:36:12,506 --> 01:36:14,426
that's going to be deprecated soon as well.

673
01:36:15,186 --> 01:36:18,346
That's why I'm thinking about open-weights models.

674
01:36:18,526 --> 01:36:22,386
I haven't played with those, but I'm pretty sure with those, I would have to see

675
01:36:22,446 --> 01:36:23,046
how they work,

676
01:36:24,426 --> 01:36:26,006
to see if they report the same thing.

677
01:36:26,106 --> 01:36:28,626
But Gemini 2, I think it's 2.1,

678
01:36:29,466 --> 01:36:33,386
and GPT-4o match in their hesitation

679
01:36:33,486 --> 01:36:36,666
scores so far. So there's hope.

680
01:36:37,506 --> 01:36:37,846
Thank you.

681
01:36:38,026 --> 01:36:38,126
Yeah.

682
01:36:40,706 --> 01:36:42,286
We can have another question.

683
01:36:46,666 --> 01:36:47,586
Anybody has a question?

684
01:36:49,146 --> 01:36:51,386
Then we will continue with the next speaker-

685
01:36:51,466 --> 01:36:51,726
Okay. Thank you

686
01:36:51,746 --> 01:36:53,486
... Yann Ondoh. Thank you very much, Greta.

687
01:36:53,486 --> 01:36:53,726
Thanks.

688
01:37:16,506 --> 01:37:17,806
Can you hear me okay? Yeah.

689
01:37:19,226 --> 01:37:21,666
So I've got a lot of things to say, but not much time.

690
01:37:21,766 --> 01:37:25,386
So bear with me if I fly over a couple of slides

691
01:37:25,666 --> 01:37:26,546
towards the end.

692
01:37:27,786 --> 01:37:30,846
So my name is Yann Ondoh from UNEP-WCMC,

693
01:37:31,566 --> 01:37:35,326
and today I'll be presenting some work I've been doing on

694
01:37:35,426 --> 01:37:39,326
using large language models to extract biodiversity knowledge

695
01:37:39,926 --> 01:37:43,666
from taxonomic literature. And more specifically,

696
01:37:46,406 --> 01:37:50,226
I will show how to extract that information and

697
01:37:50,286 --> 01:37:52,766
turn it into a structured evidence

698
01:37:54,106 --> 01:37:57,326
knowledge base for species habitat preferences.

699
01:38:00,166 --> 01:38:01,666
So I'm not going to start

700
01:38:02,706 --> 01:38:06,606
by talking about AI, but by talking about the enormous

701
01:38:06,686 --> 01:38:10,366
amount of biodiversity knowledge that has been accumulated over

702
01:38:10,426 --> 01:38:11,066
centuries.

703
01:38:13,086 --> 01:38:16,546
So natural history collections are

704
01:38:17,046 --> 01:38:20,846
product of centuries of explorations and

705
01:38:20,906 --> 01:38:23,866
collections of species specimens.

706
01:38:25,586 --> 01:38:29,486
Observations about species, where they were found,

707
01:38:29,766 --> 01:38:30,146
also

708
01:38:31,626 --> 01:38:33,786
the environment in which they occurred.

709
01:38:36,726 --> 01:38:38,066
A lot of that information

710
01:38:39,786 --> 01:38:43,406
has been compiled into a huge amount of

711
01:38:43,766 --> 01:38:47,706
taxonomic knowledge and has an example

712
01:38:47,826 --> 01:38:48,046
in

713
01:38:49,266 --> 01:38:50,826
Floras, faunas,

714
01:38:51,746 --> 01:38:54,426
checklist, and taxonomic literature.

715
01:38:56,766 --> 01:38:59,706
So there's a huge source of ecological knowledge,

716
01:39:02,686 --> 01:39:05,726
but much of it is embedded into text.

717
01:39:06,566 --> 01:39:10,306
And one useful piece of information in this literature

718
01:39:11,426 --> 01:39:12,666
is taxonomic treatment.

719
01:39:13,626 --> 01:39:14,806
So taxonomic treatment

720
01:39:16,186 --> 01:39:19,706
brings together a lot of information about species.

721
01:39:20,726 --> 01:39:24,566
For example, here is taxonomic treatment for Dalbergia menoides.

722
01:39:25,606 --> 01:39:27,346
Sorry, talking a bit too loud.

723
01:39:28,906 --> 01:39:32,326
And in taxonomic treatments that contain a lot of information

724
01:39:32,586 --> 01:39:36,546
besides taxonomy, we also contain information about the species

725
01:39:36,586 --> 01:39:37,306
distributions

726
01:39:39,946 --> 01:39:43,866
of interest for us, habitat information, but also the

727
01:39:43,906 --> 01:39:47,726
phenology and diagnosis, so comparison with other

728
01:39:47,806 --> 01:39:50,246
species, and also morphological descriptions.

729
01:39:55,206 --> 01:39:58,746
So through Treatment Bank, Plazi has been doing a

730
01:39:58,806 --> 01:40:01,226
tremendous amount of work making

731
01:40:02,106 --> 01:40:04,646
that literature machine accessible.

732
01:40:05,966 --> 01:40:09,386
So now we have over 1.2 million taxonomic treatments

733
01:40:10,806 --> 01:40:14,466
from about almost

734
01:40:15,146 --> 01:40:17,986
94,000 articles.

735
01:40:19,666 --> 01:40:23,526
So it's good to have that literature

736
