02:03:07,818 --> 02:03:10,198
Good morning. I'm Pyrian, a research associate from Q.

1121
02:03:10,498 --> 02:03:13,598
This is joint work with Nikki Nicholson and the APNI team at Q.

1122
02:03:14,038 --> 02:03:16,978
I'm going to be talking about a pipeline we've built that uses large language

1123
02:03:17,038 --> 02:03:20,998
models for Nomenclature Act extraction into more structured records

1124
02:03:21,038 --> 02:03:21,438
for APNI.

1125
02:03:23,694 --> 02:03:27,394
First, some context about the International Plant Name Index,

1126
02:03:27,774 --> 02:03:31,573
otherwise known as IPNI. So IPNI is the nomenclature index of vascular

1127
02:03:31,653 --> 02:03:35,474
plant names published under the ICN, so the International Code of Nomenclature for

1128
02:03:35,574 --> 02:03:39,234
algae, fungi, and plants. For each name, it records the

1129
02:03:39,334 --> 02:03:42,854
author's type specimen, place, date of publication, and much more

1130
02:03:42,914 --> 02:03:46,154
information extracted from these taxonomic publications.

1131
02:03:47,554 --> 02:03:51,394
It currently holds over 1.4 million plant names drawn from

1132
02:03:51,434 --> 02:03:54,974
18,500 publications by over 55,000

1133
02:03:55,054 --> 02:03:58,754
authors. It is curated and maintained by the Plant and

1134
02:03:58,834 --> 02:04:00,094
Fungal Names team at Kew.

1135
02:04:02,454 --> 02:04:06,354
As an important data source for global taxonomic backbones such as GBIF,

1136
02:04:06,834 --> 02:04:10,694
any errors in IPNI would propagate downstream, so the information

1137
02:04:10,734 --> 02:04:12,074
needs to be highly accurate.

1138
02:04:15,174 --> 02:04:19,134
Now, new treatments are published continuously across hundreds of journals,

1139
02:04:19,294 --> 02:04:22,074
and a single paper might describe several new species,

1140
02:04:22,914 --> 02:04:24,314
combinations, or status changes.

1141
02:04:24,674 --> 02:04:28,294
And getting this information out currently relies on manual curation of very dense

1142
02:04:28,334 --> 02:04:32,154
text, which is the big problem. Why? The text is difficult.

1143
02:04:32,374 --> 02:04:35,894
It's abbreviation-heavy, it's partly in Latin, and again,

1144
02:04:36,514 --> 02:04:39,774
the formatting is inconsistent across journals and publishers.

1145
02:04:40,214 --> 02:04:41,734
There is no shared structure to exploit.

1146
02:04:42,834 --> 02:04:46,234
Because there's no shared structure to exploit, rule-based parsing doesn't

1147
02:04:46,274 --> 02:04:46,734
generalize.

1148
02:04:48,294 --> 02:04:50,774
What does this mean? This means the curation is expensive.

1149
02:04:51,174 --> 02:04:55,154
So to give you a scale, hundreds of publications are screened every year by

1150
02:04:55,214 --> 02:04:58,174
just a handful of editors. It is labor-intensive.

1151
02:04:58,214 --> 02:05:02,154
It's slow, mainly because it has to be highly accurate, because errors

1152
02:05:02,174 --> 02:05:04,434
propagate downstream into resources like GBIF and POHa.

1153
02:05:04,934 --> 02:05:08,494
This combination of unstructured input and a very high accuracy bar is exactly the

1154
02:05:08,514 --> 02:05:10,374
problem we can solve using a large language model.

1155
02:05:11,154 --> 02:05:15,094
So the solution is a two-stage large language model pipeline that basically

1156
02:05:15,134 --> 02:05:17,794
takes in the PDF, converts it into a markdown,

1157
02:05:18,614 --> 02:05:21,074
cleans it for any tables or images,

1158
02:05:21,874 --> 02:05:25,514
finds where the treatments begin in the anchor detection section, and then we

1159
02:05:25,594 --> 02:05:27,614
send those treatments to the

1160
02:05:28,434 --> 02:05:32,233
model for a more structured extraction, which is then output as our

1161
02:05:32,394 --> 02:05:33,634
CSV and JSON files

1162
02:05:34,634 --> 02:05:35,954
used for curator review.

1163
02:05:38,234 --> 02:05:39,654
So pre-processing step is simple.

1164
02:05:40,554 --> 02:05:44,314
We take the input PDF and convert into markdown using the PyMuPDF Python

1165
02:05:44,374 --> 02:05:48,334
library. We then clean out images, image captions, and tables,

1166
02:05:49,754 --> 02:05:50,974
because these are kept out

1167
02:05:52,274 --> 02:05:56,014
optionally from the anchor and extraction steps, so they don't pollute them.

1168
02:05:57,354 --> 02:06:01,194
In parallel, we also retrieve the publication metadata from Crossref,

1169
02:06:02,854 --> 02:06:06,334
and with a fallback to default publication details if

1170
02:06:06,894 --> 02:06:09,834
no DOI is found. On the next step, we take the

1171
02:06:10,894 --> 02:06:14,234
section text, and we send it to the large language models to be scanned for

1172
02:06:14,274 --> 02:06:16,974
treatment boundaries. So this will be

1173
02:06:17,934 --> 02:06:20,934
basically the taxon name, the nomenclature act, pad with

1174
02:06:21,514 --> 02:06:25,134
authors and any additional information such as figure references or table

1175
02:06:25,174 --> 02:06:26,434
references and so on and so forth.

1176
02:06:27,354 --> 02:06:31,334
The model proposes candidates, which we then ground against

1177
02:06:31,374 --> 02:06:35,094
the source document. So basically, we do an exact match first

1178
02:06:35,174 --> 02:06:39,054
against lines in the document, and then we fall back to fuzzy matching if it

1179
02:06:39,094 --> 02:06:40,294
doesn't match.

1180
02:06:41,674 --> 02:06:45,634
And this is done to validate, and this is validated against a similarity

1181
02:06:45,694 --> 02:06:46,974
threshold of

1182
02:06:49,094 --> 02:06:49,514
90%.

1183
02:06:51,014 --> 02:06:54,894
This means an anchor only survives if it corresponds to a real span of text in

1184
02:06:54,934 --> 02:06:57,734
the document rather than something the model has reconstructed.

1185
02:06:59,134 --> 02:07:00,534
And thus, we have the outputs

1186
02:07:01,714 --> 02:07:05,594
treatments to send to the large language model again for extraction, which...

1187
02:07:06,554 --> 02:07:09,674
So in this step, each of these treatment blocks is then sent to the model

1188
02:07:09,734 --> 02:07:11,834
individually with the detailed extraction prompts.

1189
02:07:12,274 --> 02:07:16,134
Sending them one at a time keeps the context small, so no dense treatment

1190
02:07:16,254 --> 02:07:20,034
can bleed into the next. The output is also constrained with a finite

1191
02:07:20,094 --> 02:07:22,094
schema we've defined with over 30 fields.

1192
02:07:22,474 --> 02:07:26,354
The fields grouped into the following: the taxonomy, basin of

1193
02:07:26,394 --> 02:07:29,954
information, holotypes and isotype information, collection data,

1194
02:07:30,714 --> 02:07:33,634
geographical information, and optionally, descriptions.

1195
02:07:35,334 --> 02:07:38,834
And if a response doesn't validate against the schema, we log the failed

1196
02:07:38,874 --> 02:07:42,694
extraction, and we carry on. So one bad treatment doesn't just take

1197
02:07:42,734 --> 02:07:43,553
down the whole paper.

1198
02:07:46,254 --> 02:07:49,914
The post-processing step, we basically take the extract records and merge them with

1199
02:07:49,934 --> 02:07:52,034
the publication record. We cross-check

1200
02:07:52,854 --> 02:07:56,474
for basic names against IPNI if possible, and we fill it in,

1201
02:07:57,054 --> 02:08:01,014
and we then output a JSON/CSV path, with CSV being easier to view

1202
02:08:01,114 --> 02:08:02,334
for curators.

1203
02:08:03,354 --> 02:08:07,154
As you can see, we have a table of five new noteworthy

1204
02:08:07,274 --> 02:08:09,474
species, all from one paper that's been extracted.

1205
02:08:10,014 --> 02:08:13,574
Family name is missing because that's not part of the text that was sent to the

1206
02:08:13,614 --> 02:08:13,814
model.

1207
02:08:15,294 --> 02:08:18,414
And everything else that's relevant has been extracted.

1208
02:08:20,994 --> 02:08:24,094
So the pipeline is model-agnostic by design through LangChain.

1209
02:08:24,254 --> 02:08:27,874
We support two serving routes, Hugging Face transformers for more local,

1210
02:08:28,014 --> 02:08:31,994
smaller models, so you can run on your own device, or vLLM

1211
02:08:32,094 --> 02:08:32,474
models,

1212
02:08:35,154 --> 02:08:38,994
so you can host larger models like the Qwen 3.8 27 billion parameter

1213
02:08:39,054 --> 02:08:40,594
model on an HPC.

1214
02:08:42,474 --> 02:08:46,094
We've used the Qwen 3.8 model hosted on GRC

1215
02:08:46,254 --> 02:08:46,714
HPC

1216
02:08:47,894 --> 02:08:51,834
for our experiments. The initial experiment we did, the early experiments were on

1217
02:08:51,894 --> 02:08:54,894
smaller models, like 7 billion parameter instruct models.

1218
02:08:55,934 --> 02:08:59,874
And these showed two problems. One, an increased risk of hallucination.

1219
02:09:00,314 --> 02:09:03,494
Two, a reduced reliability on long, complex treatment blocks.

1220
02:09:03,994 --> 02:09:07,714
So we moved towards more larger vLLM models to improve the

1221
02:09:07,734 --> 02:09:09,794
extraction reliability at scale.

1222
02:09:12,874 --> 02:09:16,634
So we have a concrete example here of the source text on the left

1223
02:09:16,794 --> 02:09:18,434
and the output

1224
02:09:19,534 --> 02:09:20,854
